{
  "about": "The 3D overworld (world/overworld/; enabled false brings back the painted map everywhere): the whole travel map (data/travel, Wickermoor Hollow) raised as land on Terrain3D (addons/terrain_3d, MIT). window is the part of the travel map's picture it covers ([x0, y0, x1, y1] in the map's 0-1 positions; now all of it); size is that window's width in metres (1 world unit = 1 m here, not a 5 ft square: the overworld has no battle grid) and step the metres between two samples of the land (2 keeps the bake small; Terrain3D's vertex_spacing is the same). Each place's position comes from the travel map; `places` here only says how the land lies round it. lie: hollow (a bowl it sits in), hill (a rise it stands on; top is its base height, rise how far above it), flat (height 12, or `height`), fields (open farmland: `radius` the farmyard, `fields` the width of the plough land, stripes of `stripe` metres), isle (dry ground in a swamp, `rise` metres over the water), forest (`radius` the clearing, `forest` the dark woods round it). Extras on any place: lights (warm lamps), crags (`dir` the way the rock rises, `rise`, `reach`), scree (bare stones that far round), prairie (open grass that far round), clear (how far the woods are cleared), mist (drifting low fog: count, radius, height, alpha) and wall (a stone wall round it: half sizes, gate on the south). relief.lowlands flatten and lower the hills round a point (drop 0-1). waters are lakes: a basin (radius, depth) whose surface is a disc; swamps are low flat wet ground (base height, amp the noise in metres) with a flat water plane at `level`, so pools fill the dips. Roads follow the travel map's roads between places. trees and plants are the game's own plant models (art/plants) by how many per hectare of woods, open ground or a named zone (swamp, fields, forest, prairie). rocks are Poly Haven's scanned rocks (art/sourced/polyhaven_forest).",
